    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.bfs_layers - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.bfs_layers.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.bfs_layers()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Group nodes by their hop distance from one or more sources.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.bfs_layers()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Group nodes by their hop distance from one or more sources.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.bfs_layers()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Group nodes by their hop distance from one or more sources.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nGroup nodes by their hop distance from one or more sources.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L69468-L69559){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.bfs_layers(\n    sources,\n    mode=\"out\",\n)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![BFS layers](../assets/graphs/bfs-layers.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"An eight-node graph searched from two sources at opposite ends, A and H. Each node is colored by its hop distance to the nearer source: A and H form layer 0 (accent), B, C, F, G layer 1 (teal), and D, E, where the two searches meet, layer 2 (violet). The card lists the three layers.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nLayer 0 holds the sources, layer 1 every node one hop away, layer 2 the nodes first\nreached at two hops, and so on: the rings a breadth-first search explores. With several\nsources, each node lands in the layer of its distance to the *nearest* source.\n\nLayers are useful for drawing a graph in concentric levels, for \"friends of friends\"\nstyle queries (layer 2), and as a fast, unweighted counterpart of\n`multi_source_shortest_path_length()`. Nodes that cannot be reached appear in no layer.\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[sources]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [Union[int, str, list[Union[int, str]]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   One starting node or a list of them, as integer IDs or string labels.\n\n<code>[mode]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [str]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [[&quot;out&quot;]{.st}]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Which way to follow edges on a directed graph: `\"out\"` (default), `\"in\"`, or\n    `\"all\"`. Ignored for undirected graphs.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[list[int]]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The layers in order, each listing node IDs in discovery order.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If a node does not exist or `mode=` is invalid.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~bfs`) \n\n:   The order the search visits the nodes in.\n\n[](`~descendants_at_distance`) \n\n:   The nodes at one distance only.\n\n[](`~multi_source_shortest_path_length`) \n\n:   The distance to the nearest source, with weights.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nFrom one end of a path, each layer holds one node; from both ends, the layers meet in\nthe middle:\n\n::: {#5203026e .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\npath = ec.Graph().add_path_graph(6)\n\npath.bfs_layers(1), path.bfs_layers([1, 6])\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n([[1], [2], [3], [4], [5], [6]], [[1, 6], [2, 5], [3, 4]])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Stops from the end of the line\n\n`metro()` is a made-up city's metro network. Starting from the stations at the ends of\nlines, those with a single neighbor, the layers count the stops from the nearest end:\n\n::: {#96d8885f .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nmetro = ec.metro()\nnodes = metro.get_node_df()\nends = nodes.filter(nodes[\"degree\"] == 1)[\"id\"].to_list()\n\nlayers = metro.bfs_layers(ends)\n[[metro.get_label_from_id(n) for n in layer] for layer in layers]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n[['Docks', 'Shipyard', 'Airport'],\n ['Harbor', 'Business Park'],\n ['Riverside', 'Eastfield'],\n ['Central', 'Market', 'Old Town', 'Lakeside'],\n ['Museum', 'Opera', 'Mill Lane', 'Cathedral', 'Garden'],\n ['University', 'Westgate', 'Northpark'],\n ['Hillcrest']]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#2d906880 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nstops = {n: i for i, layer in enumerate(layers) for n in layer}\nmetro.set_node_attrs(\"stops_from_end\", stops)\nmetro.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"stops_from_end\", color_scale=\"sequential\",\n    label_by=\"label\",\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--